<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">arbalist</ansi>

The Arbalist is a crossbow shooter built to bring down armored foes. Its
Piercing Bolt hits far harder than an arrow and ignores armor, but winding
the crossbow afterwards takes its next turn, so it shoots every other turn.
Against one heavily armored foe it is strongest; against many light foes it
is weaker than a ranger. It has no creed: no alignment gate stands in front
of any of its routes.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What the Arbalist is</ansi>

  Health      4 to start, 0.75 a level to level 20 (<ansi fg="command">help health</ansi>)
  Skill       Attack 1.0 and Evasion 0.8 a level (<ansi fg="command">help evasion</ansi>)
  Gear        medium armor, no shield, crossbows only
              (<ansi fg="command">help armor</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help shields</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help equipmenttiers</ansi>)
  Stats       perception 4, strength 3, vitality 2, speed 1 (<ansi fg="command">help growth</ansi>)
  Starter kit a hunting crossbow, a padded jack, a leather cap, worn boots,
              food, a waterskin and a knapsack
  Utility     none: a shooter's work is in the fight

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Ranks of the Arbalist</ansi>

  Level 1   <ansi fg="yellow">Piercing Bolt</ansi>   the Arbalist's whole turn, once every 2 combat rounds: one
            bolt at 140% of an ordinary shot that ignores half the foe's
            armor. The turn after it is spent winding the crossbow: no
            shot, though the action meter still fills.
  Level 3   <ansi fg="yellow">Steady Aim</ansi>      a bolt has +10 Attack when nothing has hurt the Arbalist
            (no blow has landed on it) since its last bolt.
  Level 6   <ansi fg="yellow">Crippling Bolt</ansi>  a bolt that lands hobbles the foe for 2 rounds
            (<ansi fg="command">help statuses</ansi>).
  Level 8   <ansi fg="yellow">Armor-breaker</ansi>   a Piercing Bolt ignores all of the foe's armor.

Level 5 brings the first talent: Keen Edge, Footwork, Toughness, Sharp Eye
(+3% critical chance on every blow) or Heavy Bolts (a Piercing Bolt deals
10% more damage) (<ansi fg="command">help talents</ansi>). At level 10 an Arbalist promotes to a
Siegebreaker, Sharpshooter or Warden of the Wall (<ansi fg="command">help arbalist-routes</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">The winding</ansi>

A Piercing Bolt needs a shooting weapon in hand. After one, the Arbalist's
next turn is spent winding the crossbow: it makes no blow that turn. The
round it winds, the bolt's cooldown is also running, so the next bolt is
ready the round after. The battle shows both: "looses a heavy bolt" and
"winds the crossbow". A battle's end loads the crossbow again, so every
fight starts with a bolt ready. Any other weapon it holds shoots plainly.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">How an Arbalist fights</ansi>

A battle plays out on its own, so everything above happens without a command.
The Arbalist's default target rule is <ansi fg="command">armored</ansi>: the foe with the most armor it
can reach, the weakest on a tie (<ansi fg="command">help strategy</ansi>). Put it in the back row, where a
crossbow reaches any foe in a column (<ansi fg="command">help formation</ansi>), with a guardian
in front of it (<ansi fg="command">help guardian</ansi>); it makes no blow while winding. Armor reduces
a blow by a share, so the heavier the foe's armor, the more a bolt gets
through that an arrow would not. <ansi fg="command">strategy me abilities off</ansi> turns the bolt, and its
winding, off: the Arbalist then shoots every turn at an ordinary rate.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Other Arbalists</ansi>

An Arbalist companion fights the same way, by its strategy, and promotes
and chooses talents like any other (<ansi fg="command">help company</ansi>). Recruits are hired like
any other and arrive with a hunting crossbow.

Commands: <ansi fg="command">archetype</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">class</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">talent</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">strategy</ansi>.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help arbalist-routes</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help abilities</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help classes</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help promotion</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help talents</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help archetype</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>.
